[00:36.64]W: Today's special guest is Lord Blackwell, one of our country's most experienced and distinguished career diplomats.
[00:44.62]W: Welcome!
[00:45.64]M: It's my pleasure.
[00:47.30]W: Our audience will be most interested in learning about a career in the foreign service.
[00:52.56]W: However, before we talk about that, I'd like to ask you about the current developments hitting the news today regarding the Republic of Winopia.
[01:01.80]W: What can you tell us?
[01:03.40]M: I'm afraid I don't know much beyond what we've all seen on the news.
[01:07.46]M: It looks like the government of Winopia seems to be undergoing some sort of crisis of legitimacy, and an interim administration has been transitionally established until a head of state is agreed on.
[01:19.68]M: But my career work has mostly focused around the continental spheres of Europe and the Americas, and thus I've never been involved with Winopian relations.
[01:30.08]M: It's for this reason that I don't know anything on this matter beyond what we can all read in the newspapers.
[01:36.14]W: Oh, I see.
[01:37.62]M: Nevertheless, be rest assured that our expert envoys at our consulate there will be closely monitoring the situation, and I expect our bilateral relations with that country will not be affected.
[01:50.32]W: So how does one get a career in foreign relations?
[01:53.54]M: There are many different paths one can take.
[01:57.08]M: All diplomats have their own story.
[01:59.16]M: But in my case, I got a degree in electrical engineering followed by six years' service in the air force.
[02:06.18]M: Then I went to law school, and upon obtaining a degree in international law, I joined the diplomatic corps where I have been for 22 years.
[02:14.58]W: What would you say are the key skills one needs for the job?
[02:18.32]M: There are so many.
[02:20.62]M: Being a hard worker and a good student is important.
[02:23.56]M: You never stop learning.
[02:25.06]M: In addition, a diplomat needs to be very articulate in their discourse and rhetoric.
[02:30.02]M: Whether it is defusing attention or enacting policy, people and communication skills are vital.
